  {
   "role": "agent",
   "text": "Thanks, Marcus. I've confirmed your identity and looked into order K-8501, but I'm not able to give compensation for this one.\n\nThe original promised time was 7:00 PM. At 6:10 PM we sent you a push notification and a text saying the restaurant was running behind and moving the estimated time to 7:25 PM. Since that update reached you well ahead of the original time, 7:25 PM became the promised time.\n\nOur records show the courier handed over your order at 7:35 PM. That's 10 minutes after the updated time. Late-delivery credit starts when an order arrives more than 15 minutes after the promised time, so this order doesn't qualify.\n\nI'm sorry the wait was longer than you first expected. If anything else went wrong, like a missing or wrong item, tell me and I'll look into it."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Hmm, I did get a text around 6:10 saying it was running behind, but I didn't realize that moved the promised time to 7:25. It still showed up 35 minutes after the 7:00 time I ordered for, so that's a bit frustrating. Can you tell me why it doesn't count?"
  },
  {
   "role": "agent",
   "text": "That's frustrating, and I understand why. Ordering for 7:00 and getting it at 7:35 feels like a 35-minute delay.\n\nHere's why it doesn't count as late. If we warn you about a delay at least 30 minutes before the original promised time, the new estimate becomes the time we measure against. The thinking is that an early warning lets you plan around the delay, for example by eating something first, changing plans or cancelling if the order hadn't been picked up yet. Your text came at 6:10 PM, 50 minutes before 7:00, so it met that requirement. Your order was then measured against 7:25 PM.\n\nMeasured that way, it arrived 10 minutes late. We only give late-delivery credit once an order is more than 15 minutes past the promised time, so I can't issue credit for this one.\n\nI know that's not what you hoped to hear, and I'm sorry the evening didn't go as planned. If anything was wrong with the food itself, I'm happy to look into that."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Okay, I get the logic now, even if it's not what I wanted to hear. I didn't realize that text had moved the time, so I was measuring against the wrong number. The food itself was fine, so I don't have anything else to raise. Thanks for explaining it."
  },
  {
   "role": "agent",
   "text": "You're welcome, Marcus, and thanks for being so understanding. It's an easy thing to miss. In future, if you get a text saying your order is running behind, the new time in that message is the one we go by.\n\nI'm glad the food was good. Enjoy the rest of your evening, and reach out anytime if you need help."
  }
